enchilada: display: SwapEnd does not wait for the frame; a write lock on the surface leaving the panel does

Boot 29 (sweetzc=1 sweetfb_fit=1, CPU renderer): 8-14 swaps/s while swiping, each SwapEnd blocked
until a frame took its CTL flush (avg 8.9 ms, max 17 ms), because CoreAnimation draws the next
frame into the other of its two pool surfaces, which the pipe scans until then. The wait now sits
where the danger is: IOSurfaceLock for writing on a pool surface (SweetSurface trapLock, after the
0220 GPU wait) waits, at most 50 ms, for the frame that takes a pending flip away from that surface,
and SwapEnd returns right after writing the flush. The flip is finished (surface it replaced let go,
SMMU fault check, 50 ms limit) by the next flip, the write lock or the vsync tick; one flip is in
flight at a time. This is only safe while CoreAnimation write-locks what it draws, so it is learnt:
SwapEnd keeps waiting until 8 in-place swaps in a row named a surface write-locked since it was last
shown, and once relied upon, a surface presented without such a lock (other than a re-show of the
one on the panel) turns it off for good (GL composition draws without a lock: it never turns on).
Failures restore ABL's registers as before and the tick copies the latest frame. No new memory:
CoreAnimation makes two canvas surfaces and has no notion of them being busy, so a third pool slot
would go unused (and cont_splash_region has no room for one beside ABL's frame). The 10 s line now
shows swaps/s, frames in place (and how many SwapEnd did not wait for), the waits for a frame (at a
write lock; avg/max) and the pool slots in use. sweetzc_async=0 keeps the 0251-0254 behaviour.

